WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.337 --> 00:00:05.839
Uh oh.

00:00:05.839 --> 00:00:08.466
You just fell into a pool.

00:00:08.466 --> 00:00:11.052
With this thing.

00:00:19.936 --> 00:00:23.106
This is WHAT IF, and
here's what would happen

00:00:23.106 --> 00:00:27.068
if you fell into a spent nuclear fuel rods.

00:00:27.068 --> 00:00:31.114
This is a spent nuclear fuel pool.

00:00:31.114 --> 00:00:33.950
And you've just accidentally fallen into it.

00:00:33.950 --> 00:00:37.954
But what exactly is this thing?

00:00:37.954 --> 00:00:41.541
This large pool of water
is meant to cool spent fuel rods

00:00:41.541 --> 00:00:44.127
after they come out of a nuclear reactor. 

00:00:44.127 --> 00:00:49.507
While powering a nuclear reactor,
these fuel rods become very, very hot.

00:00:49.507 --> 00:00:52.469
We're talking 2,800°C (5,092°F).

00:00:52.469 --> 00:00:56.222
After it's spent 3 to 6 years inside of a nuclear reactor,

00:00:56.222 --> 00:00:58.808
a fuel rod is no longer efficient.

00:00:58.808 --> 00:01:02.437
Problem is, it still emits plenty of harmful radiation,

00:01:02.437 --> 00:01:05.815
and it will for the next 10,000 years.

00:01:05.815 --> 00:01:10.779
Even though it's trash, there's not
a garbage dump in the world that will accept it.

00:01:10.779 --> 00:01:15.658
So, why is water a good 
place to put these things?

00:01:15.658 --> 00:01:19.829
Not only does the water spend several
decades cooling the fuel rods, but

00:01:19.829 --> 00:01:22.207
it also affects their radiation. 

00:01:22.207 --> 00:01:26.002
The water essentially acts as a biological shield

00:01:26.002 --> 00:01:30.590
with hydrogen absorbing and
deflecting the radiation bouncing against it. 

00:01:30.590 --> 00:01:36.054
This makes it completely safe for you
to stand near the pool with no ill effects.

00:01:36.054 --> 00:01:39.474
But what happens if you fall into the water?

00:01:39.474 --> 00:01:44.938
As soon as you orient yourself, you'll
quickly notice the temperature of the pool.

00:01:44.938 --> 00:01:49.359
This big tub of water won't be the refreshing
dip you're looking for on a hot summer day.

00:01:49.359 --> 00:01:52.445
Instead, it'll be around 30°C (86°F).

00:01:52.445 --> 00:01:57.283
So a bit colder than your
average hot tub but still very warm.

00:01:57.283 --> 00:02:01.496
Let's hope you didn't accidentally turn
off the water flow as you're falling into the pool. 

00:02:01.496 --> 00:02:04.749
If you did, then you'd be in some serious trouble.

00:02:04.749 --> 00:02:08.503
Spent nuclear fuel pools
are constantly cycling out water

00:02:08.503 --> 00:02:11.172
in order to keep it at a cool enough temperature. 

00:02:11.172 --> 00:02:14.342
Remember these fuel rods are incredibly hot.

00:02:14.342 --> 00:02:16.511
And if the water isn't cycled out, then

00:02:16.511 --> 00:02:20.390
it would heat up significantly
and slowly evaporate over time.

00:02:20.390 --> 00:02:23.893
Resulting in you, burning to a crisp.

00:02:23.893 --> 00:02:28.857
But that didn't happen, so now that
you're starting to swim in this hot pool of water,

00:02:28.857 --> 00:02:31.943
when does the horrible
radiation poisoning kick in?

00:02:31.943 --> 00:02:35.738
Or when do you turn green
and start to gain superpowers?

00:02:35.738 --> 00:02:37.699
These are the sort of things
that are supposed to happen

00:02:37.699 --> 00:02:40.785
when you fall into something
that has nuclear fuel in it right?

00:02:41.286 --> 00:02:42.328
Not exactly.

00:02:42.328 --> 00:02:44.080
Although that sounds like fun,

00:02:44.080 --> 00:02:47.834
it wouldn't happen if you were to
fall into a spent nuclear fuel pool. 

00:02:47.834 --> 00:02:50.003
That's because of the water.

00:02:50.003 --> 00:02:53.214
Not only does it protect
the people outside of the pool.

00:02:53.214 --> 00:02:57.260
It'll also protect you if you happen to fall inside one.

00:02:57.260 --> 00:03:01.139
As long as you don't swim several
meters underwater to touch the fuel rods,

00:03:01.139 --> 00:03:02.849
you'd be completely fine.

00:03:02.849 --> 00:03:05.727
In theory, you could swim in this pool

00:03:05.727 --> 00:03:09.564
until you looked like a raisin
without feeling any negative effects.

00:03:09.564 --> 00:03:12.942
Although they're protecting
something incredibly dangerous,

00:03:12.942 --> 00:03:17.155
spent nuclear fuel pools
are quite serene and kind of boring. 

00:03:18.573 --> 00:03:23.369
In fact, this might be a lot cleaner
and safer than your average public pool.

00:03:23.369 --> 00:03:27.498
You won't find any germs
or children peeing in the water here.

00:03:27.498 --> 00:03:33.504
Swimming in this pool would expose you to less
radiation than you experience in your everyday life.

00:03:33.504 --> 00:03:38.468
We encounter radiation in medical
procedures such as X-rays and CT scans. 

00:03:38.468 --> 00:03:41.471
From certain fuels like gas or coal;

00:03:41.471 --> 00:03:46.809
even our televisions and other electronic
screens give off tiny traces of radiation.

00:03:46.809 --> 00:03:52.941
Swimming in here isn't any more
dangerous than watching a WHAT IF video.

00:03:52.941 --> 00:03:57.987
If splashing around in a spent
nuclear fuel pool is too boring for you,

00:03:57.987 --> 00:04:02.575
how about swimming on one of Saturn's moons?

00:04:02.575 --> 00:04:08.865
That sounds like a story for another WHAT IF.

